      <p dir="auto"><span class="issue-keyword tooltipped tooltipped-se" aria-label="This pull request closes issue #5811.">Closes</span> <a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="677390392" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5811" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/whatwg/html/issues/5811/hovercard" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5811">#5811</a>.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Mini-explainer</h3>
<p dir="auto">This new element allows authors to express the semantics of "a set of form controls or other content related to performing a search or filtering operation".</p>
<p dir="auto">This is an especially desired semantic because it is something that an ARIA landmark role exists for (<code class="notranslate">role="search"</code>), but today can only be expressed with ARIA. Adding a dedicated element allows authors to follow the <a href="https://w3c.github.io/using-aria/#rule1" rel="nofollow">first rule of ARIA</a> (~ "don't use ARIA if you can avoid it") and is part of the <a href="https://w3c.github.io/aria/#co-evolution" rel="nofollow">co-evolution process</a> between ARIA and HTML.</p>
<p dir="auto">Please see <a href="https://whatpr.org/html/7320/grouping-content.html#the-search-element" rel="nofollow">the pull request preview</a> for web-developer focused details on usage and examples.</p>
<p dir="auto">Some notable design choices:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>
<p dir="auto">The name. We were initially a bit concerned that developers would think that this was a type of search input, not a search container. Although we briefly discussed alternative names to combat this like <code class="notranslate">&lt;searcharea&gt;</code>, <code class="notranslate">&lt;searchform&gt;</code>, <code class="notranslate">&lt;searchgroup&gt;</code>, <code class="notranslate">&lt;searchset&gt;</code>, they each had problems, and a quick <a href="https://twitter.com/domenic/status/1437891692926017537" rel="nofollow">Twitter poll</a> helped seal the deal that developers would expect and prefer the name <code class="notranslate">&lt;search&gt;</code>. (In addition to other developers chiming in with reasoning on the thread.) See the comments starting at <span class="reference"><svg data-component="Octicon" class="octicon octicon-issue-closed closed mr-1" title="Closed" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M11.28 6.78a.75.75 0 0 0-1.06-1.06L7.25 8.69 5.78 7.22a.75.75 0 0 0-1.06 1.06l2 2a.75.75 0 0 0 1.06 0l3.5-3.5Z"></path><path d="M16 8A8 8 0 1 1 0 8a8 8 0 0 1 16 0Zm-1.5 0a6.5 6.5 0 1 0-13 0 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 13 0Z"></path></svg><a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="677390392" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5811" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/whatwg/html/issues/5811/hovercard?comment_id=916489175&amp;comment_type=issue_comment" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5811#issuecomment-916489175">Consider creating an HTML search element<span class="issue-shorthand"> #5811 (comment)</span></a></span> for more details.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir="auto">Like other "block-level" elements, <code class="notranslate">&lt;search&gt;</code> closes any open <code class="notranslate">&lt;p&gt;</code> tags. This ensures an easy transition path from <code class="notranslate">&lt;div role="search"&gt;</code> to <code class="notranslate">&lt;search&gt;</code> and maintains platform consistency.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir="auto">Like other "block-level" elements, the user-agent stylesheet for <code class="notranslate">&lt;search&gt;</code> makes it <code class="notranslate">display: block</code> and <code class="notranslate">unicode-bidi: isolate</code>.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir="auto">For conformance-checker purposes, the element is categorized as flow content and palpable content, but not sectioning content. (In this respect, it is categorized similarly to <code class="notranslate">&lt;main&gt;</code>, but not like <code class="notranslate">&lt;nav&gt;</code> or <code class="notranslate">&lt;aside&gt;</code>.) We are not sure whether this is ideal, partly because due to <span class="reference"><svg data-component="Octicon" class="octicon octicon-issue-closed closed mr-1" title="Closed" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M11.28 6.78a.75.75 0 0 0-1.06-1.06L7.25 8.69 5.78 7.22a.75.75 0 0 0-1.06 1.06l2 2a.75.75 0 0 0 1.06 0l3.5-3.5Z"></path><path d="M16 8A8 8 0 1 1 0 8a8 8 0 0 1 16 0Zm-1.5 0a6.5 6.5 0 1 0-13 0 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 13 0Z"></path></svg><a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="104343495" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/83" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/whatwg/html/issues/83/hovercard" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/83">Suggest adding a warning about outline algorithm<span class="issue-shorthand"> #83</span></a></span> sectioning content is a tricky category. But we believe that the main effect of sectioning content is to encourage authors to add headings inside the sections, which is perhaps not the right default here?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p dir="auto">This element is a straightforward replacement for <code class="notranslate">&lt;div role="search"&gt;</code>, and does not come with any additional functionality (such as having form-submission capabilities). Instead it can be combined with other primitives such as <code class="notranslate">&lt;form&gt;</code>, or JavaScript, etc. You can see extensive discussion of this in <span class="reference"><svg data-component="Octicon" class="octicon octicon-issue-closed closed mr-1" title="Closed" viewBox="0 0 16 16" version="1.1" width="16" height="16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M11.28 6.78a.75.75 0 0 0-1.06-1.06L7.25 8.69 5.78 7.22a.75.75 0 0 0-1.06 1.06l2 2a.75.75 0 0 0 1.06 0l3.5-3.5Z"></path><path d="M16 8A8 8 0 1 1 0 8a8 8 0 0 1 16 0Zm-1.5 0a6.5 6.5 0 1 0-13 0 6.5 6.5 0 0 0 13 0Z"></path></svg><a class="issue-link js-issue-link" data-error-text="Failed to load title" data-id="677390392" data-permission-text="Title is private" data-url="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5811" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/whatwg/html/issues/5811/hovercard" href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5811">Consider creating an HTML search element<span class="issue-shorthand"> #5811</span></a></span>. The reasons for disfavoring more complex solutions are <a href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5811#issuecomment-1012650363" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/whatwg/html/issues/5811/hovercard">implementer-expressed considerations about complexity and compat risk</a>, as well as <a href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/5811#issuecomment-981747584" data-hovercard-type="issue" data-hovercard-url="/whatwg/html/issues/5811/hovercard">web-developer and accessibility community preferences</a>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
